Role of the Project Manager
1. Introduction
- The project manager leads the project team and makes sure that project goals are achieved.
- They are involved in the project from the beginning to the end.
- Before starting a project, they discuss with top managers to understand business goals and customer needs.
- In some organizations, project managers also help with business analysis, business plans, and portfolio management.
- Their role may change depending on the organization and the project.
- They balance project needs with available resources.
2. Project Manager as a Communicator
- The project manager communicates with team members, sponsors, and stakeholders.
- They explain project goals and how success will be achieved.
- They use communication and people skills to solve problems and get everyone to agree.
- Consensus means reaching an agreement among stakeholders about project decisions.
Main communication responsibilities:
1. Communicate clearly through speaking, writing, and body language.
2. Prepare and follow communication plans and schedules.
3. Share information regularly and correctly.
4. Understand the communication needs of stakeholders.
5. Keep messages short, clear, simple, and relevant.
6. Share both good news and bad news.
7. Allow stakeholders to give feedback.
Importance: Good communication helps the project run smoothly and achieve success.
3. Project Manager as a Coordinator
- The project manager coordinates project activities with different managers and teams.
- They work with the project sponsor to solve organizational and strategic problems.
- They make sure project activities are connected with organizational goals.
- They work with the PMO, functional managers, program managers, business analysts, and experts.
- They make sure the project plan matches the organization's overall plans.
Main coordination responsibilities:
1. Help the project sponsor understand business goals.
2. Make sure project goals match organizational goals.
3. Bring people, processes, and knowledge together.
4. Guide the team to focus on important tasks.
5. Coordinate resources and activities.
6. Adjust plans when project needs change.
Importance: Good coordination helps the team work together and achieve project goals.
4. Project Manager as a Representative
- The project manager represents the project when communicating with different people and groups.
- They build good relationships with people who can support the project.
- They use these relationships to solve problems and overcome organizational difficulties.
Types of networks:
- Formal networks: Official relationships in an organization, such as managers and reporting structures.
- Informal networks: Personal relationships with experts, leaders, and other important people.
Main responsibilities:
1. Build and maintain good relationships.
2. Communicate with other project managers.
3. Find and share resources with other projects.
4. Coordinate funding, people, and technical resources.
5. Manage conflicts when different projects need the same resources.
6. Make sure project goals match organizational goals.
Importance: Good relationships help the project manager get the support and resources needed to complete the project successfully.

